[Music]  my name is marin borkad i'm professor  for digital entrepreneurship and  innovation management at the exo  exponential university for applied  science and i'm coordinating the  research for the fair work project  the fair work project is funded by the  berlin university alliance and it's  carried out here the technical  university of berlin together with the  oxford internet institute and humboldt  university  and it's about the platform economy  so the platform economy is a reasoned  and rapidly growing phenomenon  um in 2008 it's generated worldwide with  the platform economy worldwide generated  in revenue about 204 billion us dollars  and this is going to double in just five  years okay  the pandemic has shown that demand for  platform work has actually increased in  only um  five months from january to may 2020  the demand for platform work rose by 40  percent  and of course with regard to the  lightning of the regulations you know we  see a small decline but nevertheless  it's going to be more and more important  and people are going to be engaged in it  so what is really platform work think  about all the workers that deliver the  service that you book online okay so  there are two broad categories of  platform work one is a category that is  geographically tethered  to in-person services so it requires a  geographical proximity of the worker and  the customer these are household related  services like cleaning caring pet  sitting this is food delivery every time  you order something for lunch at home or  for dinner there's a person bringing you  the the service bringing you the food  this is related to ride-hailing services  in the city when you book yourself  a cab to the main station right  so these are geographic tethers and this  is what we call platform work besides of  this there's crowd work crowd work is  different crowd work is a work that can  be done online  from everywhere in the world and this is  related to services just as  filling in a survey or training in an  eye system or doing  doing  questionnaires  doing design work doing translational  stuff all these kind of things this is  crowd work and you already realize that  the rights of workers the risks they  encounter are pretty different between  the two major types of platform work  and fair work is focusing on the  platform work that is locally bounded  that is an in-person delivery of a  service booked online  well the main actors of the platform  economy  usually people think about well there  are two sides right there the workers  delivering a service and they're the  customer demanding the services and the  platform is just something like the  technological tool that facilitates the  interplay between supply and demand yet  these platforms are not naturally given  they are human made  they have  they are based on business values  business choices  they reveal relevant insights into  business goals and business strategies  so these um platforms the digital  platforms they really provide the basic  conditions the framework under which  the workers and the customers meet  so they are playing a very important  part in the platform economy as well  for many people for many new scammers in  the city and in this country it's an  easy way to earn it's a fast-track way  to earn money to earn themselves an  income it's a lifeline for those who  face barriers in accessing regular  employment  and very often  so it's attractive for migrants  extractive also for students for example  right and um very often um um both  starts as considering platform work as a  kind of interim solution and a kind of  transitional  stage but then they realize that  due to the barriers they face in  accessing the regular labour market they  stick with platform work and the  disadvantages though  are related to  employment rights very often digital  platform  circumvent or try to to  to  to prevent  workers from accessing rights these are  employment rights like minimum wages  sick pay  regulations with regard to working hours  or even  collective bargaining rights right  but there are also rights related to  social protection like unemployment  benefits  and how do they do this well what we see  in the digital economy in the platform  economy is that they classify workers or  they misclassify workers even sometimes  as independent workers um  letting them assume all the  responsibility  for their health insurances and so forth  so forth so this is something really  that we should think about is this the  new kind of digital entrepreneurship  yeah in the platform economy in the  digital age are these the kind of  working conditions that we all are going  to have to face in the digital age um  are working conditions in germany  deteriorating  or can germany due to its strong labor  regulations  act as a vanguard  to tame for other countries and vanguard  to other countries also in order to tame  the most excessive  expressions of platform capitalism  well the fair work project is uh  focusing on working conditions working  conditions of platform workers and  it's meant to gather insight into this  recently developing and very  powerful new new type of economy  and it's also meant to provide practical  suggestions for how to improve the  working conditions of platform workers  so the fair work project is based on  five basic principles and we are going  to um to provide together information  data with regard to these five  principles and we are going to come up  with a ranking that is related to these  five principles they are pay fair  conditions fair contract fair management  and fair representation so the first two  are related really to the fair payment  that the platform worker receives and to  working conditions that are sales and  protecting their workers health  the third um  principle is related to  the digital platform providing really a  contract and not just terms and  conditions that is often happening in  the geek economy in the platform economy  and fair representation is included in a  management that allows for open  communication channels that establishes  processes um clear and transparent  processes for communications also  between a customer and worker and the  fifth is about  fair representation  and here we refer to collective  bargaining rights to rights of the  workers in order to form the  council and and have a collective body  to show and communicate their concerns  and their necessities  well we have a dream at fair work to be  honest we would like to engage with the  workers the customers  the regulators and the companies  themselves the companies providing  setting up these digital platforms  in order to come up with a fairer  future for the gig economy the platform  economy in germany and the way we are  trying to achieve this is we are  gathering data with workers interviews  with desk research  with this data we are  convening management interviews and we  are going to talk to the owners and  establishers of these digital platforms  about our findings right and we are  going to ask them with regard to fair  pay with regard to fair representation  with regard to fair management what can  you do what kind of data can you provide  us so that we could grant this extra  point  for you and why are we doing this  because we do believe that  research  has an impact in society  so the gather that we are the data that  we are gathering does not belongs to us  into a publication but it also belongs  to the companies in order to provide  them the possibility to improve their  services to improve how they relate with  their with their platform workers right  so and it's also there in order to  inform customers because each of the  customers we all have a choice when we  are ordering food or when we are booking  a ride to the next  station or a party or whatever we have a  choice which platform we are going to  choose and which kind of services we are  going to book so it's all about  transparency and about raising awareness  and getting people into collaborating  towards establishing a fairer gig  economy in general  [Music]  so the fair web project is actually um  the bua you know the berlin university  alliance is made of a consortium of  three universities and this is the  technical university of berlin the  oxford internet institute and the  humboldt university and the three  institutions collaborate together in  order to implement the fairway project  besides this of course fair work germany  is only one small part in an overall  picture  so the fair work project is actually  carried out in 28 different countries  all around the world because the  platform economy is not only a new  phenomenon in germany but it's relevant  in all of the countries around the world  so we have colleagues all over the world  in 28 countries we are collecting data  with regard to  to the working conditions of platform  workers and the idea is of course also  to compare them  the principles is something that we are  going to update every year okay so the  principles with regard to the rankings  of the different platforms are something  that has been  has been developed together with the ilo  and other the unions and so on so forth  you know these institutions who  traditionally are engaged and have very  good experience and and expertise when  it comes to workers right right um but  they have been discussed and and and  worked out for an analog  economy now we are in a digital economy  so we have to take these working  standards these working rights and find  a translation for this new type of  economy right so at the beginning we had  the workshop with these established  institutions  in order to come up with the principles  that are then going to be implemented in  all of the countries and around the  world we just talked about 28 countries  and  with every year  we have rounds of revisions when it  comes to the principles  it's very important to understand that  these are general principles something  like fair pay you know this is a general  principle of course it's related to  the local minimum wage why local because  it changes between country and country  and city and city  besides this we understand that also  what is recognized as the minimum wage  differs from country to country this is  why we established also the living wage  which is actually something more  concrete in terms of the workers life  experience to understand if the money  that he or she earns is really enough to  make it until the end of the month right  so we have these principles that are  going to be contextualized in each of  the countries and the cities that we are  investigating and they are going to be  rise on a yearly basis with regard to  the experiences of all the different  research teams around the world  [Music]  yeah just just  take a step outside  what you're going to see is the gorillas  rider you're going to see the uber  drivers  what you're not going to see are the  services provided  by people  in the households right that are going  to be booked through digital platforms  what i'm trying to say is that um  i think corona has even shown it more  the pandemic has really shown it um  digital infrastructures are part of our  everyday lives um  we  book so many different services through  them right from ordering lunch  or  ordering a ride to the main station  so it's part of our daily lives i think  the pandemic has even shown this more  it's important that we that we  acknowledge that this is  a growing part of the economy  um  there are no  comprehensive or  consistent comparative data when it  comes to how many people are actually  working in the geek economy in the  platform economy in germany  but nevertheless we are talking about  estimates talk about from 500 000 to 1.6  million people  or almost three percent of the working  age populations so this is a this is a  constant number and it's growing  it's important to focus on this topic  because  what we can see from and what we can  hear from the streets from the media is  that a lot of these platform workers um  they are not satisfied with their  working conditions  um you could see it in the recent prose  tests right of riders here in berlin  that make it to the media  last year there was  the amendment of the  transportation services personal  transportation services with regards to  opening up this very established taxi  industry towards new forms of  right-handling uber and so on so forth  free now  so there's a lot of happening in our  cities and a lot of happening in our  economy and society and this is  important to acknowledge it's not only  an economic phenomenon it's also a  social phenomenon because there's people  who are  affected by the working conditions  so the aim of the fair work project is  really to gather information  and approach platforms with this  information in order to provoke change  so there's a couple of platforms who do  like to collaborate with us  who um who are improving on their  working conditions on the working  conditions of the workers in order to  render a service okay to them in order  to improve their situation  from last year's report one of the  effects of impacts of last year's report  is that two of the digital platforms  that we investigated they're in  corporate anti-discrimination guidelines  to to their own business models um so  and this is the kind of impact that we  would like to see because they realized  that a lot of their workers they do have  a migration background so it's important  also to kind of  highlight these issues that may arise  from not being fluent in german having a  contract in another language other than  german not being familiar with certain  rights and the systems in and employment  benefits in germany and so on so forth  so it's very important that platforms  realize  what kind of workforce is working for  them and what kind of needs they do have  and again for this year's protests of  course the fair work researchers were  also present  we observed we  we collected data we collected data also  from the workers  and we are going to approach also the  management and going to ask them what  they can do in order to improve working  conditions okay because this is the  overall goal and impact that we are  striving for  [Music]  so  [Music]  you

